A set is an unordered collection of unique elements.

Unlike lists:
    A set does not allow duplicates.
    A set does not preserve indexing (you cannot access elements by position).
    Sets are optimized for fast searching.

A set automatically removes duplicates.

Sets are extremely fast for checking whether an item exists.


pop() -It removes a random element, not the "first" or "last".
Since sets are unordered, you cannot predict which item is removed.


Frozen Sets - A normal set is mutable.
frozenset is useful when you want an immutable set, but it's used less often than regular sets.

--- Quick Revision Notes ---
A set is used to store unique values.
It does not keep order, so you cannot access items by index.
Duplicates are removed automatically.

Common Set Methods:
- add(value) → adds one element
- remove(value) → removes an element; gives an error if missing
- discard(value) → removes an element; does not give an error
- pop() → removes a random element
- clear() → removes all elements

Common Set Operations:
- union: A | B → all unique elements from both sets
- intersection: A & B → common elements
- difference: A - B → elements in A but not in B
- symmetric difference: A ^ B → elements in one set but not both

Conversion:
- list → set: set(numbers)
- set → list: list(unique_set)

Important Note:
- {} creates an empty dictionary, not an empty set
- Use set() to create an empty set

Example:
fruits = {"Apple", "Banana", "Orange"}
fruits.add("Mango")
print(fruits)

Remember:
Sets are useful when you want to remove duplicates and check membership quickly.


